<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
<Fragment set:html={"<section class=\"article-hero\"><div class=\"shell\"><div class=\"breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/resources/\">Resource Center</a> / <a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Building Guides</a> / 21</div><div class=\"eyebrow\">HOMEOWNER GUIDE 21 \u00b7 Budget &amp; Buying</div><h1>Pre-Purchase Checklist</h1><p>Use one final review to confirm the important details\n              before approving the building and moving forward.</p></div></section><div class=\"shell deep-layout\"><article><section class=\"deep-answer\" id=\"overview\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">THE ESSENTIAL ANSWER</div><h2>What should you know before choosing?</h2><p>A premium backyard building should not move forward on excitement alone. Before purchase approval, the use, site, approvals, materials, written scope, price, warranty, maintenance responsibilities and change-order process should be clear enough to protect both the homeowner and the project.</p><p>This guide is the final confirmation before the project moves from planning into approval. It helps you verify what is being built, where it will sit, what is included, what is excluded, what could change the price, what approvals may be needed and what responsibilities remain after completion.</p></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-1\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 01</div><h2>The essentials</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>What to confirm before you approve your backyard building project.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A premium backyard building should not move forward on excitement alone. Before purchase approval, the use, site, approvals, materials, written scope, price, warranty, maintenance responsibilities and change-order process should be clear enough to protect both the homeowner and the project.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>This guide is the final confirmation before the project moves from planning into approval. It helps you verify what is being built, where it will sit, what is included, what is excluded, what could change the price, what approvals may be needed and what responsibilities remain after completion.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-2\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 02</div><h2>What Should You Confirm Before Buying a Backyard Building?</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The project should be understandable without relying on assumptions, verbal promises or a model photograph.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Before approving a backyard building, confirm the intended use, correct size, site location, drainage, access, foundation approach, permit or HOA questions, material standards, included features, exclusions, comfort-system planning, written price, warranty terms, maintenance responsibilities and change-order process.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A good pre-purchase checklist answers practical questions: What is being built? Where will it sit? Who is responsible for approvals? What is included? What is excluded? What could change the price? What must the owner do after completion? What warranty applies?</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-3\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 03</div><h2>Confirm These Categories Before You Approve the Build</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The purpose of the final checklist is to make the purchase decision clear, written and specific to your property.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm Use, Size and Layout</h3><p>Confirm the primary use, secondary use, realistic future use, overall size, walking space, storage zones, work areas, furniture, equipment, window placement and door placement.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm Site, Access and Drainage</h3><p>Review placement, access route, gate width, slope, drainage, downspouts, soil concerns, foundation area, work-zone clearance and long-term maintenance space.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm Permit, HOA and Utility Questions</h3><p>Identify exact-address questions involving zoning, permits, setbacks, easements, HOA or architectural review, utility marking, private underground lines and any trade requirements that may apply to the project.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm Materials and Options</h3><p>Confirm the floor system, framing, siding, roof, doors, windows, trim, ventilation, ramps, lofts, porch details, exterior finish expectations and every selected upgrade.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-4\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 04</div><h2>Six Areas Should Be Clear Before Purchase Approval</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Excitement about the finished building is valuable. Clarity about the project is essential.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Use Controls the Building</h3><p>Storage, workshop, office, studio, garden use, pool support or retreat use can require different layouts, doors, windows, utilities, insulation and finishes.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>The Site Controls the Build</h3><p>Drainage, slope, access, utilities, easements, setbacks and work-zone clearance can change what is practical, what is priced and when the project is ready.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>The Written Scope Controls Expectations</h3><p>Scope, exclusions, price assumptions, changes, schedule, warranty and owner responsibilities should not depend on memory.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Ownership Controls Long-Term Success</h3><p>Warranty, maintenance, finish responsibilities, drainage, vegetation and project documentation should be understood before the building enters its first season.</p><p>A clear decision is better than a fast decision. The buyer should feel informed rather than pressured. If a major detail still feels vague, that detail should be clarified before purchase approval.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-5\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 05</div><h2>The Hidden Decisions Should Be Clear Before the Visible Building Appears</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Final purchase approval should confirm more than exterior appearance.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-6\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 06</div><h2>The Most Expensive Assumptions Often Happen Right Before Approval</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The final checklist helps you slow down long enough to catch details that protect both the property and the project.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Buying Before the Use Is Clear</h3><p>A beautiful structure can still miss the mark if storage, work, office, studio, garden, pool-support or retreat needs have not been defined.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Assuming the Site Is Ready</h3><p>Drainage, grade, access, utilities, easements, setbacks and maintenance clearance should be reviewed before construction is scheduled.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Forgetting Approval Responsibility</h3><p>Permit, zoning, HOA, utility and trade responsibilities should be clarified for the actual property and project scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confusing Options With Included Features</h3><p>Doors, windows, siding upgrades, porches, ramps, lofts, electrical, HVAC, insulation and interior finishing should be clearly identified as included, optional or excluded.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-7\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 07</div><h2>How to Tell Whether the Project Is Ready for Approval</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A homeowner can be enthusiastic about a project before the project itself is fully defined.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-8\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 08</div><h2>Know What You Should Confirm\u2014and What Requires Outside Approval</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A purchase checklist helps organize decisions. It does not replace the authority of local jurisdictions, HOAs, utility owners or licensed trades.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Homeowner &amp; Builder Confirmation</h3><p>Preferred placement and orientation</p><p>Written price and payment expectations</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Authority / Trade Confirmation</h3><p>Zoning and permit requirements where applicable</p><p>HOA or architectural-review requirements</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-9\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 09</div><h2>Final Questions That Protect the Project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>These questions should have clear answers before the project moves from planning into purchase approval.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>What Exactly Am I Buying?</h3><p>The answer should identify more than a model name.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>What Is Not Included?</h3><p>Exclusions are one of the most common places for misunderstandings.</p><p>Site preparation unless included in writing</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Is the Site Ready?</h3><p>Confirm practical construction and long-term site conditions.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>What Could Change the Price?</h3><p>Potential cost changes should not remain hidden assumptions.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-10\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 10</div><h2>Confirm These Before You Commit</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>This is the final safety check before approving the build.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Secondary and realistic future use considered</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Size has a practical reason behind it</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Approvals &amp; Construction</h3><p>Permit and zoning questions identified</p><p>HOA requirements identified where applicable</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Scope &amp; Ownership</h3><p>Warranty coverage and exclusions reviewed</p><p>Maintenance responsibilities understood</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-11\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 11</div><h2>There Is No Single Approval Path for Every Property</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Property-specific review matters because jurisdictions, neighborhoods, private restrictions, utilities and site conditions can differ throughout the region.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Public &amp; Private Approval Questions</h3><p>Confirm whether city, township, county, zoning, building-department, trade-permit or inspection requirements may apply to the actual property and project scope.</p><p>Where applicable, also review HOA rules, architectural-review requirements, deed restrictions, subdivision standards or other private requirements that may affect size, placement, siding, roofing, color or visibility.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Property &amp; Utility Questions</h3><p>Property lines, utility easements, drainage easements, access easements, conservation areas or other restricted areas can influence where a building may be practical.</p><p>Before digging or other ground disturbance, public utility-marking requirements should be followed and known private utilities\u2014including irrigation, landscape wiring, private electric, pet fencing, drainage, septic, propane or pool-related lines\u2014should be disclosed and addressed as appropriate.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-12\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 12</div><h2>The Final Decision Should Be Clear Enough to Put in Writing</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>The time immediately before purchase approval is not the time to ignore unanswered questions. It is the best opportunity to confirm what the building must accomplish, whether the property is ready and exactly what the written scope includes.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A clear project protects the homeowner, the property and the builder. If the checklist uncovers an unanswered question, resolving it before construction is far easier than discovering it after materials arrive or work begins.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-13\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 13</div><h2>Straight Answers About the Final Pre-Purchase Checklist</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Confirm the intended use and the written scope. The use helps determine the appropriate size, layout, systems and site placement. The written scope confirms what is included, excluded, priced, owner-responsible and promised.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Approval questions should be checked or identified before the project is treated as purchase-ready. Permit, zoning, HOA, setback, easement and utility requirements can affect size, placement, exterior appearance, utilities or the overall project scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>Appalachian Sheds specializes in built-on-site backyard structures. Because construction takes place on the customer's property, site readiness, access, drainage, approvals, written scope and project planning are important parts of the purchasing decision.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"topic-14\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">PART 14</div><h2>Use the Checklist Before You Approve the Build</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><p>A clear decision protects the homeowner, the property and the finished building.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Confirm the Project Before Moving Forward</h3><p>Before approving the build, confirm the use, size, site, approvals, materials, selected options, comfort planning, written scope, price assumptions, warranty and maintenance responsibilities.</p><p>If the final checklist reveals unanswered questions, that is not a setback. It is the right time to clarify the project\u2014before construction begins.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Appalachian Sheds Inc.</h3><p>Veteran &amp; Family-Owned \u00b7 35+ Years of Construction Experience</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"asi-scope\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">IMPORTANT PROJECT DISTINCTION</div><h2>How this relates to an Appalachian Sheds project</h2><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Built on your property</h3><p>All Appalachian Sheds buildings are constructed on site. Property access, drainage, topography, location restrictions and the selected model can affect planning.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Exterior shell versus finished interior</h3><p>Our standard building proposal describes a weather-tight exterior shell. Insulation, electrical, plumbing, heating, cooling, and interior wall or ceiling finishes are separate work unless expressly listed in the written scope.</p></div><div class=\"deep-point\"><h3>Only the written scope is authoritative</h3><p>Model-specific specifications, pricing, selected options, finishing requirements and any engineering or permit obligations must be confirmed for the actual building and site. Avoid treating this general guide as an engineering design or a permit determination.</p></div></section><section class=\"deep-section\" id=\"next\"><div class=\"deep-eyebrow\">MAKE THE NEXT DECISION</div><h2>Your next planning step</h2><p>Compare this decision with the relevant model features and options, then request a written proposal identifying standard inclusions, any optional work and property-specific requirements.</p><div class=\"related\"><a href=\"/buildings/\">View Models &amp; Pricing</a><a href=\"/planning-guides/\">Explore Building Guides</a><a href=\"/options-upgrades/\">Browse Options &amp; Upgrades</a></div></section></article><aside class=\"deep-toc\"><strong>On this page</strong><a href=\"#overview\">Essential answer</a><a href=\"#topic-1\">The essentials</a><a href=\"#topic-2\">What Should You Confirm Before Buying a Backyard Building?</a><a href=\"#topic-3\">Confirm These Categories Before You Approve the Build</a><a href=\"#topic-4\">Six Areas Should Be Clear Before Purchase Approval</a><a href=\"#topic-5\">The Hidden Decisions Should Be Clear Before the Visible Building Appears</a><a href=\"#topic-6\">The Most Expensive Assumptions Often Happen Right Before Approval</a><a href=\"#topic-7\">How to Tell Whether the Project Is Ready for Approval</a><a href=\"#topic-8\">Know What You Should Confirm\u2014and What Requires Outside Approval</a><a href=\"#topic-9\">Final Questions That Protect the Project</a><a href=\"#topic-10\">Confirm These Before You Commit</a><a href=\"#topic-11\">There Is No Single Approval Path for Every Property</a><a href=\"#topic-12\">The Final Decision Should Be Clear Enough to Put in Writing</a><a href=\"#topic-13\">Straight Answers About the Final Pre-Purchase Checklist</a><a href=\"#topic-14\">Use the Checklist Before You Approve the Build</a><a href=\"#asi-scope\">How this relates to an ASI project</a><a class=\"btn light\" href=\"/buildings/\">See Your Price</a></aside></div>"} />
</Base>

